The setting was searched with a batched GPU port of the program (about 30000 runs scored against the
scans, the best rerun here with the Fortran program). The score is the 2 px agreement both ways with
Figures 14 and 15 (deskewed, the frame lines 3.75 px from the contours as calibrated on the uniform
inlet flow, the downstream x scale 2/3 of the upstream one, fixed by the 5.4h label), less twice the
distance from the report's Figure 13 curve. What the search shows:

  y spacing: a uniform y grid matches far worse (0.59/0.47 against 0.74/0.75); the report's was not
  uniform. On a non-uniform grid the wall distance in DIRCOS is taken to the interior neighbour,
  not to the wall node (walldist corrects it; little effect here, Xr +0.03h).
  Single changes on a fixed grid: C1 = 1.44 (the report's table; the program has 1.43) Xr +0.11h;
  nolip, no wall function at the node behind the step corner, where the separating shear layer
  starts, Xr +0.13h and the k peak from 0.069 to 0.056; genshear, production from the shear terms
  only, Xr +0.16h; wallk and wallkt change nothing. Together the four overshoot (5.67h).
  Searched together with the grid (each change a switch of the run), the best matches all use
  nolip and none use C1 = 1.44, walldist or genshear: Xr = 5.31h. Held at Xr = 5.4h, the search
  turns genshear on and matches a little worse (Figure 14 0.77 against 0.79, Figure 15 0.70
  against 0.71). The hybrid factor goes to about 0.8 (0.5 printed).
  Inlet profile (1/7 power, boundary layers) and the inlet turbulence change the streamlines little.
  Chen and Kim (NASA CR-179204, 1987, the same author, the elliptic solver of this report with
  second-order upwind differencing, 51x41 grid, grid independent within 1 percent) give 5.2h for
  the standard k-epsilon model on this step.

